Frequently Asked Questions
What is the ASA classification?
ASA I: healthy patient, non-smoker, no comorbidities. ASA II: mild controlled systemic disease (e.g., controlled hypertension, BMI 30-35, smoking). ASA III: severe systemic disease with functional limitation (e.g., poorly controlled diabetes, COPD, BMI > 40, dialysis-dependent CKD). ASA IV: severe systemic disease with constant threat to life (e.g., sepsis, cardiogenic shock, recent MI). ASA V: moribund patient, not expected to survive > 24h without surgery. ASA VI: declared brain death (organ donor). The letter "E" can be added (e.g., ASA IIE) for emergency surgeries, which have 2-3x higher risk.
Bibliographic References
AMERICAN SOCIETY OF ANESTHESIOLOGISTS. ASA Physical Status Classification System. 2020. Available at: asahq.org
ALDRETE, J. A.; KROULIK, D. A postanesthetic recovery score. Anesthesia &amp; Analgesia , v. 49, n. 6, p. 924-934, 1970.', 'publicado'),
('/en/escalas-de-enfermagem/dor/index.html', 'en', 'secao', 'Pain Scales: FLACC, CRIES, BPS | Pain Assessment in Nursing', 'Complete guide to pain assessment scales: FLACC, CRIES, BPS, and Numeric Scale. Validated instruments for adults, children, neonates, and non-verbal critical patients.', 'Home
Nursing Scales
Pain
PAIN ASSESSMENT
Pain Assessment Scales
Validated instruments for pain assessment in adult, pediatric, critically ill, and non-verbal patients.
Quick Navigation
Introduction When to Use Comparison Flowchart Scales Related FAQ References
Introduction to Pain Assessment
A pain has been recognized as the 5th vital sign since the 1990s, and its systematic assessment is a patient''s right and a nursing team''s duty. However, pain is a subjective and multidimensional experience — making its measurement a challenge, especially in populations that cannot communicate verbally.
In conscious and communicative adult patients , the Numeric Pain Scale (0-10) is the gold standard: simple, quick, and with excellent correlation with pain intensity. But what when the patient is a pre-verbal child , a newborn , um elderly with advanced dementia or is under mechanical ventilation in the ICU ? For each of these situations, there are validated behavioral scales.
O FLACC (Face, Legs, Activity, Cry, Consolability) is the most versatile behavioral scale — applicable from infants to elderly with cognitive impairment. The CRIES was developed specifically for neonatal postoperative pain. And the BPS (Behavioral Pain Scale) is the instrument of choice for critically ill patients under mechanical ventilation and sedation.
Adequate pain assessment is the first step toward effective management. The Brazilian Society for the Study of Pain (SBED), the International Association for the Study of Pain (IASP), and COFEN recommend systematic documentation of pain intensity as a quality-of-care indicator.
When to Use Each Scale
Population / Situation Recommended Scale Justification
Communicative adult Numeric Scale (0-10) Gold standard. Quick. Excellent correlation with pain intensity. Self-report is the best pain indicator.
Child aged 2 months to 7 years FLACC (0-10) Behavioral. Does not require language. Assesses 5 observable parameters. Broad international validation.
Postoperative newborn CRIES (0-10) Specific for neonatal pain. Assesses crying, O2, vital signs, expression, and sleep. Validated from 32 weeks to 6 months.
ICU patient on mechanical ventilation BPS (3-12) Assesses facial expression, upper limbs, and ventilator adaptation. Does not require verbal response. BPS > 5 = consider analgesia.
Elderly with dementia FLACC or PAINAD FLACC adapted for non-verbal adults. PAINAD specific for dementia (breathing, vocalization, expression, body language, consolability).
Extreme premature ( PIPP Considers gestational age, HR, SpO2, brow bulge, eye squeeze, and nasolabial furrow. Gold standard in neonatal ICU.
Comparison of Pain Scales
Scale Population Range Parameters Assessed
Numeric (ENVD) Adults and children > 8 years 0-10 Self-report of pain intensity (0 = no pain, 10 = worst imaginable pain)
FLACC 2 months to 7 years; any age non-verbal 0-10 Face, legs, activity, cry, consolability (each 0-2)
CRIES Neonates (32 weeks to 6 months) 0-10 Crying, O2 for SpO2 > 95%, increased HR/BP, expression, sleep (each 0-2)
BPS Adult ICU on mechanical ventilation 3-12 Facial expression (1-4), upper limbs (1-4), adaptation to ventilation (1-4)
NIPS Neonates (procedures) 0-7 Facial expression, crying, breathing, arms, legs, state of alertness
