Frequently Asked Questions
How to assess pain in an intubated ICU patient?
The BPS (Behavioral Pain Scale) is the recommended scale for mechanically ventilated patients. It assesses: facial expression (relaxed=1, tense=2, grimace=3, grimace with closed eyes=4), upper limb movements (absent=1, partial flexion=2, full flexion with finger flexion=3, permanent flexion=4), and adaptation to mechanical ventilation (tolerant=1, coughs but tolerates=2, fights the ventilator=3, unable to control=4). Total score 3-12. BPS > 5 has 80% sensitivity for significant pain and indicates need for analgesia. Assessment should be performed before and after painful procedures to monitor analgesic efficacy.
Which pain scale to use in newborns?
For term newborns: CRIES (Crying, Requires O2 for SpO2 > 95%, Increased vital signs, Expression, Sleepless) for postoperative pain — score 0-10, each item 0-2. NIPS (Neonatal Infant Pain Scale) for acute procedures (0-7). For extreme prematures ( What does FLACC mean and when to use it?
FLACC stands for Face (0=neutral to 2=frowning grimace), Legs (0=relaxed to 2=kicking), Activity (0=quiet to 2=rigid), Cry (0=no cry to 2=intense cry), and Consolability (0=comfortable to 2=difficult to console). Each item 0-2, total 0-10. It is the most versatile behavioral scale: originally validated for children aged 2 months to 7 years (Merkel, 1997), but widely adapted for any non-verbal patient — elderly with dementia, patients with cognitive impairment, ASD, post-stroke with aphasia. FLACC 0 = no pain; 1-3 = mild; 4-6 = moderate; 7-10 = severe. It should be applied at rest and during procedures.
Bibliographic References
MERKEL, S. I. et al. The FLACC: a behavioral scale for scoring postoperative pain in young children. Pediatric Nursing , v. 23, n. 3, p. 293-297, 1997.
KREICHEL, S. W.; BILDNER, J. CRIES: a new neonatal postoperative pain measurement score — initial testing of validity and reliability. Pediatric Anesthesia , v. 5, n. 1, p. 53-61, 1995. DOI: 10.1111/j.1460-9592.1995.tb00242.x
PAYEN, J. F. et al. Assessing pain in critically ill sedated patients by using a behavioral pain scale. Critical Care Medicine , v. 29, n. 12, p. 2258-2263, 2001. DOI: 10.1097/00003246-200112000-00004
STEVENS, B. et al. Premature Infant Pain Profile: development and initial validation. The Clinical Journal of Pain , v. 12, n. 1, p. 13-22, 1996. DOI: 10.1097/00002508-199603000-00003', 'publicado'),
('/en/escalas-de-enfermagem/funcionalidade/index.html', 'en', 'secao', 'Functionality Scales: Barthel and ADL | Nursing', 'Guide to functionality scales: Barthel Index for assessing Activities of Daily Living (ADL). Functional independence in rehabilitation, geriatrics, and neurology.', 'Home
Nursing Scales
Functionality
FUNCTIONALITY
Functionality Scales
Instruments for assessing functional independence in Activities of Daily Living (ADL).
Quick Navigation
Introduction When to Use Assessed ADLs Scales Related FAQ References
Introduction to Functional Assessment
A functionality is the individual''s ability to perform daily activities independently. In nursing, functional assessment is a central component of geriatric consultation, neurological rehabilitation, and hospital discharge planning. It determines the degree of assistance needed, eligibility for rehabilitation programs, and recovery prognosis.
O Barthel Index , created by Mahoney and Barthel in 1965, is the most widely used functional scale in the world. It assesses 10 Activities of Daily Living (ADL) — feeding, bathing, dressing, grooming, toileting, transfers, ambulation, and stairs — generating a score from 0 (total dependence) to 100 (complete independence). Its simplicity, reproducibility, and sensitivity to change make it the instrument of choice in rehabilitation clinical trials.
Functional assessment not only documents the patient''s current status but also allows monitor progress — a change of 5-10 points on the Barthel is considered clinically significant. The score directly correlates with length of hospital stay, need for institutionalization after discharge, and mortality in the elderly.
When to Use the Barthel
Situation Objective
Admission to rehabilitation unit Establish functional baseline and plan interventions.
Post-stroke Monitor neurological recovery. A change of 10+ points indicates significant improvement.
Elderly with functional decline Identify specific deficits and need for caregivers.
Discharge planning Barthel
Pre- and post-orthopedic surgery Assess functional gain with the intervention (hip/knee prosthesis).
The 10 ADLs of the Barthel Index
Activity Score Criterion
Feeding 0, 5, 10 10=independent; 5=needs help; 0=unable
Bathing 0, 5 5=independent; 0=dependent
Dressing 0, 5, 10 10=independent; 5=partial help; 0=dependent
Personal hygiene 0, 5 5=independente; 0=dependente
Bowel elimination 0, 5, 10 10=continent; 5=occasional incontinence; 0=incontinent
Bladder elimination 0, 5, 10 10=continent/catheter; 5=occasional incontinence; 0=incontinent
Toilet use 0, 5, 10 10=independente; 5=auxílio parcial; 0=dependente
Chair-bed transfer 0, 5, 10, 15 15=independent; 10=minimal assistance; 5=sits but does not transfer; 0=bedridden
Ambulation 0, 5, 10, 15 15=walks 50m; 10=walks 50m with help; 5=independent wheelchair; 0=immobile
Climbing stairs 0, 5, 10 10=independent; 5=with help; 0=unable
