Frequently Asked Questions
What is the Barthel Index and what does it assess?
The Barthel Index assesses the degree of functional independence in 10 Activities of Daily Living (ADL): feeding (0-10), bathing (0-5), dressing (0-10), personal hygiene (0-5), bowel elimination (0-10), bladder elimination (0-10), toilet use (0-10), transfers (0-15), ambulation (0-15), and stairs (0-10). Total score 0-100, where 0 = complete dependence and 100 = independence for all basic ADLs. It is the most widely used instrument worldwide for functional assessment, with cross-cultural validation in more than 30 countries and in Brazilian Portuguese.
How to interpret the Barthel score?
Barthel 100: independent for all ADLs. 91-99: mild dependence (needs supervision). 61-90: moderate dependence (needs help with some ADLs). 21-60: severe dependence (needs help with most ADLs). 0-20: total dependence. In clinical practice, Barthel ≥ 60 at discharge suggests the possibility of returning home with support; Barthel Bibliographic References
MAHONEY, F. I.; BARTHEL, D. W. Functional evaluation: the Barthel Index. Maryland State Medical Journal , v. 14, p. 61-65, 1965.
WADE, D. T.; COLLIN, C. The Barthel ADL Index: a standard measure of physical disability? International Disability Studies , v. 10, n. 2, p. 64-67, 1988. DOI: 10.3109/09638288809164105', 'publicado'),
('/en/escalas-de-enfermagem/geriatria/index.html', 'en', 'secao', 'Geriatric Scales: GDS, Barthel, Morse and Downton | Nursing', 'Complete guide to geriatric scales: GDS for depression in the elderly, Barthel for functionality, Morse and Downton for fall risk. Interactive calculators.', 'Home
Nursing Scales
Geriatrics
GERIATRICS
Geriatric Scales
Instruments for multidimensional assessment of the elderly: mood, functionality, fall risk, and vulnerability.
Quick Navigation
Introduction When to Use Comparison Scales Related FAQ References
Introduction to Geriatric Scales
A geriatric assessment differs from conventional clinical assessment due to its multidimensional nature: the elderly often present multiple interrelated chronic conditions that cannot be assessed in isolation. The so-called "geriatric syndromes" — falls, functional decline, depression, cognitive impairment, and frailty — require specific and validated instruments for this population.
A The Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS) , developed by Yesavage et al. in 1983, is the most widely used screening instrument for depression in the elderly. Unlike other depression scales, the GDS avoids somatic symptoms (fatigue, appetite changes, insomnia) that can be confused with the aging process itself or with clinical comorbidities. Its reduced 15-item version (GDS-15) is quick (3-5 minutes) and widely validated in Brazilian Portuguese.
A functional assessment with the Barthel Index and the fall risk assessment with Morse (hospital) or Downton (geriatric/community) complete the triad of geriatric assessment. The integration of these instruments allows identifying the elderly at risk of adverse outcomes and planning targeted preventive interventions.
When to Use Each Scale
Situation Scale Justification
Screening for depression in the elderly GDS-15 Specific for the elderly, avoids somatic symptoms. GDS > 5 = probable depression.
Falls in hospitalized elderly Morse Hospital gold standard. > 45 = high risk.
Falls in elderly in the community/LTCF Downton Simpler, specific for geriatrics. ≥ 3 = risk.
Functional decline Barthel Assesses ADLs.
Pressure injury risk Braden Age > 70 is an independent risk factor for PI.
Comparison of Geriatric Scales
Scale Domain Range Risk Cutoff
GDS-15 Mood (depressive symptoms) 0-15 > 5 = probable depression
Barthel Functionality (ADLs) 0-100
Morse Fall risk (hospital) 0-125 > 45 = high risk
Downton Fall risk (geriatric) 0-11 ≥ 3 = risk
Braden Pressure injury risk 6-23 ≤ 16 = risk
