Frequently Asked Questions
What is the Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS)?
The GDS-15 contains 15 questions with YES/NO answers about how the elderly person felt in the last week. Examples: "Are you satisfied with your life?" (NO=1), "Do you feel that your life is empty?" (YES=1). Score 0-5 is normal, 6-10 suggests mild to moderate depression, 11-15 severe depression. The great advantage over other scales (such as the Beck Inventory) is that the GDS avoids questions about somatic symptoms — fatigue, changes in appetite or sleep — that are common in normal aging. GDS > 5 should be referred for formal psychiatric/psychological evaluation.
Why is multidimensional assessment important in geriatrics?
The elderly often present interrelated "geriatric syndromes": depression increases the risk of falls, falls lead to bed rest and functional decline, functional decline worsens depression. A unidimensional assessment (only cognition or only falls) misses these interactions. Multidimensional assessment combines GDS (mood), Barthel (functionality), Morse/Downton (falls), and Braden (skin), and is the gold standard recommended by SBGG and WHO for the comprehensive approach to the elderly. The result guides an individualized care plan that addresses all domains simultaneously.
Bibliographic References
