Frequently Asked Questions
What is the difference between Ballard and Capurro?
Capurro (1978) uses 5 somatic parameters (skin texture, ear shape, mammary gland, plantar creases, and nipple formation), is faster (2-3 min) and suitable for newborns over 29 weeks. The New Ballard Score (1991) assesses 12 parameters — 6 physical (skin, lanugo, plantar surface, breast, eye/ear, and genitalia) and 6 neuromuscular (posture, popliteal angle, scarf sign, heel-to-ear, wrist flexion, and arm recoil) — being more accurate and applicable to extreme preterm infants (from 20 weeks). Ideally, it should be applied in the first 12-24h of life. In Brazilian practice, Ballard is the gold standard for neonatal ICU and preterm infants, while Capurro is often used in rooming-in for its simplicity.
What does each letter of APGAR mean?
APGAR is an acronym coined by Joseph Butterfield in 1963 in honor of Virginia Apgar: A = Appearance (skin color: cyanotic=0, acrocyanosis=1, pink=2), P = Pulse (HR: absent=0, 100=2), G = Grimace (response to stimulus: absent=0, grimace=1, cry/cough=2), A = Activity (tone: flaccid=0, slight flexion=1, active movement=2), R = Respiration (effort: absent=0, irregular=1, strong cry=2). Total 0-10. Assessment at 1st minute guides resuscitation; at 5th minute has prognostic value.
When is Apgar considered normal?
Apgar 7-10 is considered normal, indicating good adaptation to extrauterine life. Apgar 4-6 at 1 minute indicates moderate asphyxia — usually the newborn responds well to positive pressure ventilation and oxygen, reaching 7-10 at 5 minutes. Apgar 0-3 is severe neonatal asphyxia, requiring immediate resuscitation according to the SBP Guidelines (2022): positive pressure ventilation, intubation, and cardiac massage if HR Bibliographic References
