Introduction to Obstetric Scales
A obstetric nursing occupies a privileged position in the maternal-child care line: it is the link between prenatal care, childbirth, and initial newborn care. The obstetric scales are instruments that systematize clinical assessment in these three critical moments, supporting decisions that directly impact maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality.
O Bishop Score , described by Edward Bishop in 1964, is the quintessential obstetric scale. It assesses five characteristics of the cervix — dilation, effacement, consistency, position, and fetal station — to predict the likelihood of successful labor induction. A favorable Bishop score (> 8 points) is associated with a vaginal delivery rate of 80-90%, while an unfavorable Bishop score ( At the moment of birth, the transition to immediate neonatal care is mediated by the Apgar score . Although classified as a neonatal scale, its application at the 1st and 5th minute of life is inseparable from obstetric care — the obstetric nurse is responsible for the initial assessment of the newborn and must communicate the Apgar score to the team as an indicator of fetal-neonatal transition.
The scales of Ballard e Capurro complete the picture by allowing the estimation of postnatal gestational age, essential information for classifying the newborn (preterm, term, or post-term) and for defining clinical management.
When to Use Each Scale
Clinical Situation Scale Justification
Indication for labor induction Bishop Predicts induction success. Bishop > 8: direct induction with oxytocin. Bishop
Post-term pregnancy (≥ 41 weeks) Bishop Assess cervical favorability for induction. Unfavorable Bishop may indicate cesarean section.
Premature rupture of membranes Bishop Defines management: favorable Bishop → immediate induction; unfavorable → maturation + induction.
Immediate newborn assessment (1st and 5th min) Apgar Universal standard. Determines need for neonatal resuscitation.
Confirmation of gestational age Ballard / Capurro When LMP is uncertain or discordant with ultrasound. Classifies newborn for appropriate management.
