Frequently Asked Questions
What is the difference between FAST and Cincinnati?
Both are prehospital stroke screening scales with 3 items and high sensitivity (&gt;80%). FAST was developed in the UK and its acronym stands for Face, Arms, Speech, Time — where the "T" is not an assessment item but a reminder to record the time of symptom onset. The Cincinnati Scale (CPSS) was developed in the USA and assesses the same parameters with slight differences in description: asymmetric smile, unilateral arm drift, and abnormal speech. In practice, they are interchangeable — the key is that any positive result (1 or more altered items) immediately activates the stroke protocol and referral to a specialized center.
When to apply CURB-65 in the emergency department?
CURB-65 should be applied to every patient with suspected community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) in the emergency department. The 5 criteria: Confusion (altered mental status), Urea &gt; 50 mg/dL, Respiratory Rate ≥ 30 breaths/min, Blood pressure (systolic &lt; 90 or diastolic ≤ 60 mmHg) and Age ≥ 65 years. Each item = 1 point. Score 0-1: safe outpatient treatment; score 2: consider hospital admission; score ≥ 3: severe pneumonia, indication for ICU admission. Early application reduces decision time and is associated with lower mortality.
What is the nurse''s role in applying FAST?
The nurse is often the first professional to assess a patient with suspected stroke, whether in SAMU, risk classification, or the emergency department. Responsibilities include: applying FAST immediately, recording the exact time of symptom onset (or last known well), communicating the result to the medical team, preparing the patient for CT, collecting laboratory tests, checking capillary blood glucose (hypoglycemia can mimic stroke) and monitoring vital signs. After thrombolysis, the nurse applies the NIHSS every 15 minutes during infusion and for the first 2 hours, being essential to detect early neurological deterioration.
