('/en/guia_rapido_dispositivos.html', 'en', 'guia', 'Quick Guide: Invasive Devices, Catheters, and Tubes - Nursing Calculators', 'Reference guide for selection, indications, gauges, and best practices in nursing regarding vascular access devices, tubes, and drains.', 'Home
Library
Invasive Devices Guide
Quick Reference Guide
Invasive Devices, Catheters, and Tubes
Reference guide for selection, indications, gauges, and best practices in nursing.
1. Vascular Access Devices (Intravenous and Arterial)
Device Type
Gauge (G/Fr)
Dwell Time
Recommended Use/Flow
Advantages
Contraindications
Main Complications
Central Venous Catheter (CVC)
4Fr to 12Fr
(Single to Triple Lumen)
Short-term
(Days to weeks)
Vasoactive drugs, continuous sedation, TPN, CVP monitoring.
Access to large-caliber veins. Multiple independent lumens.
Severe coagulopathy, active infection at the puncture site.
Pneumothorax during insertion, central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI).
Peripheral Venous Catheter (Over-the-Needle Jelco/Abocath)
14G to 24G
72 to 96 hours
(or daily clinical assessment)
Hydration, IV medications, transfusion.
Flow: from 20 mL/min (24G) up to 240 mL/min (14G).
Flexible plastic cannula allows mobility. Rapid insertion.
Prolonged use of vesicant drugs or TPN.
Phlebitis, infiltration, extravasation, and local infection.
Needle Catheter (Scalp)
19G to 27G
Immediate use
(Minutes to a few hours)
Blood collection, rapid single-dose infusion (bolus).
Fine metal needle, excellent for difficult veins for one-time access.
Continuous infusions (high risk of damaging the endothelium).
Rapid infiltration, vein transfixation.
PICC (Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter)
2Fr to 5Fr
Long-term
(Months to >1 year)
Chemotherapy, prolonged antibiotics (>6 days), TPN, vesicant therapies.
Inserted by nurse. Lower risk of serious insertion complications.
Severe chronic renal failure (preserve venous network for fistula).
Occlusion, peripheral venous thrombosis, mechanical phlebitis.
Arterial Catheter
18G to 22G
Short-term
(Days)
Invasive Arterial Blood Pressure (IBP) monitoring, serial blood gases.
Accurate continuous BP measurement in critically ill patients.
Absence of collateral circulation (negative Allen''s test).
Distal limb ischemia, severe bleeding, thrombosis.
Implantable Venous Catheter (Port-a-Cath)
Various sizes
Very long-term
(Years)
Oncology (intermittent chemotherapy), chronic infusions.
Fully subcutaneous. Aesthetic and allows immersion baths.
Patient refusal of surgical procedure, bacteremia.
Extravasation due to puncture failure (Huber needle), pocket infection.
Hemodialysis Catheter (Shiley/Permcath)
10Fr to 14Fr
Shiley (Days)
Permcath (Months)
Hemodialysis, Hemofiltration, Plasmapheresis (High Flow: >300 mL/min).
Allows urgent renal replacement therapy.
Use for routine medication infusion (prohibited!).
Hemorrhage, severe systemic infection, central venous stenosis.
Umbilical Catheter (Newborn)
2.5Fr to 5.0Fr
Short-term
(Up to 14 days max)
Immediate access in Neonatal ICU: blood gases (arterial) and TPN (venous).
Painless for the newborn, avoids repeated peripheral punctures.
Omphalitis, necrotizing enterocolitis.
Lower limb ischemia, thrombosis, hemorrhage.', 'publicado'),
