('/en/integracoes_classificacao_wifi.html', 'en', 'secao', 'SVS WIfI Classification: Complete Guide — Nursing Calculators', 'Complete didactic guide to the Society for Vascular Surgery WIfI Classification: history, Wound-Ischemia-foot Infection criteria, amputation and revascularization risk matrices, management, and interactive calculator.', 'Home
WIfI Classification
Complete Guide
Complete Didactic Guide
Society for Vascular Surgery WIfI Classification
Wound, Ischemia, foot Infection — Risk stratification for amputation and revascularization benefit in threatened lower limbs.
WIfI Calculator
SINBAD
WIfI Evidence
SINBAD Evidence
1. Introduction
The WIfI Classification ( Wound, Ischemia, foot Infection ) is a risk stratification system developed by the Society for Vascular Surgery (SVS) of the United States for the assessment of threatened lower limbs in patients with peripheral arterial disease and diabetic foot. Originally published in 2014 in the Journal of Vascular Surgery (MILLS et al., 2014), the classification was developed by the SVS Lower Extremity Guidelines Committee with the aim of standardizing language across medical specialties — vascular surgeons, endocrinologists, infectious disease specialists, orthopedic surgeons, and nurses — and enabling objective comparisons of clinical outcomes across different centers and studies (MILLS et al., 2014, p. 221).
Unlike previous classifications that focused on only one aspect of the disease — such as the Fontaine classification (ischemia), the Wagner classification (wound), or the IDSA classification (infection) — the WIfI system integrates three independent axes that, when combined, determine the overall risk of major amputation within one year and the likelihood of benefit from revascularization (MILLS et al., 2014). Mills et al. (2014, p. 222) state that "the WIfI classification was designed to stratify the risk of amputation based on three key factors: wound, ischemia, and foot infection, each graded on a scale from 0 to 3."
2. History and Authorship
Year Event
2013 The SVS Lower Extremity Guidelines Committee , chaired by Dr. Joseph L. Mills Sr. , begins developing the integrated classification system. The committee brought together 14 experts from different institutions in the US and Europe (MILLS et al., 2014).
Jan/2014 Official publication in the Journal of Vascular Surgery (v. 59, n. 1, p. 220-234.e2) titled "The Society for Vascular Surgery Lower Extremity Threatened Limb Classification System: Risk stratification based on Wound, Ischemia, and foot Infection (WIfI)" (MILLS et al., 2014).
2014-2019 Clinical validation in multiple centers across the US, Europe, and Asia. Studies demonstrated that the WIfI clinical stage is an independent predictor of major amputation and 1-year mortality (ZETTERVALL et al., 2015; DARLING et al., 2016).
2019 The Global Vascular Guidelines (CONTE et al., 2019) endorse the WIfI classification as the international standard for risk stratification in chronic limb-threatening ischemia (CLTI).
2020-present Progressive adoption in Brazil, recommended by the Brazilian Society of Angiology and Vascular Surgery (SBACV) and incorporated into diabetic foot management protocols in Brazilian university hospitals (SBACV, 2020).
Region of origin and involved institutions
The classification was developed in the United States , led by the University of Arizona (Tucson, AZ) and involving centers such as Harvard Medical School (Boston, MA), University of California (San Francisco, CA), and Mayo Clinic (Rochester, MN). The expert panel included vascular surgeons, endocrinologists, infectious disease specialists, and wound care specialists (MILLS et al., 2014).
Countries where it is used
Country/Region Context of use
United States Origin and initial validation; SVS standard for revascularization clinical trials (MILLS et al., 2014)
European Union Endorsed by the Global Vascular Guidelines (CONTE et al., 2019); used in multicenter registries in Germany, the United Kingdom, Italy, and the Netherlands
Japan and Asia Validated in Asian populations with diabetic foot; adapted for anatomical and epidemiological differences (AZUMA et al., 2018)
Brazil Recommended by SBACV; used in university hospitals (USP, UNICAMP, UFRJ) and reference diabetic foot services (SBACV, 2020)
3. The Three Components of the WIfI Classification
The classification assesses three independent parameters, each graded from 0 to 3 , where 0 = normal/absent and 3 = severe impairment. The combination of W, I, and fI grades determines the WIfI clinical stage (1 to 4), which in turn estimates the 1-year amputation risk and the expected benefit of revascularization (MILLS et al., 2014, p. 223).
Component Grade Description
W — Wound 0 No ulcer, no gangrene (ischemic rest pain may be present)
1 Small, shallow ulcer on the distal leg or foot; no bone exposure unless limited to the distal phalanx; no gangrene or gangrene limited to the distal phalanx
2 Deep ulcer with exposed bone, joint, or tendon; generally does not involve the heel; gangrene limited to the toes
3 Extensive, deep ulcer involving the forefoot and/or midfoot; deep heel ulceration (± calcaneus); extensive gangrene involving the forefoot and/or midfoot; extensive heel necrosis
I — Ischemia 0 ABI ≥ 0.80; ankle systolic pressure > 100 mmHg; toe pressure (TP) or TcPO₂ ≥ 60 mmHg
1 ABI 0.60-0.79; ankle systolic pressure 70-100 mmHg; TP/TcPO₂ 40-59 mmHg
2 ABI 0.40-0.59; ankle systolic pressure 50-69 mmHg; TP/TcPO₂ 30-39 mmHg
3 ABI ≤ 0.39; ankle systolic pressure
fI — foot Infection 0 No signs or symptoms of infection; no evidence of active infection
1 Local infection involving only skin and subcutaneous tissue (no deep tissue involvement, no systemic signs); erythema > 0.5 cm and ≤ 2 cm around the ulcer
2 Local infection with erythema > 2 cm, or involving deep structures (abscess, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, fasciitis); without SIRS (Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome)
3 Local infection with signs of SIRS (≥ 2 criteria: temperature > 38°C or 90 bpm; RR > 20 breaths/min or PaCO₂ 12,000 or 10% band forms)
Source: Adapted from MILLS et al. (2014, p. 223-225). ABI = Ankle-Brachial Index; TP = Toe Pressure; TcPO₂ = Transcutaneous Oxygen Pressure; SIRS = Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome.
Attention: The WIfI classification should be applied by a trained professional. The interpretation of scores and the definition of clinical management — especially the indication for revascularization and amputation — are within the competence of the vascular physician (MILLS et al., 2014; SBACV, 2020).
Legend of Abbreviations
Abbreviation Meaning Abbreviation Meaning
WIfI Wound, Ischemia, foot Infection SVS Society for Vascular Surgery
W Wound I Ischemia
fI foot Infection ABI Ankle-Brachial Index
TP Toe Pressure TcPO₂ Transcutaneous Oxygen Pressure
SIRS Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome CLTI Chronic Limb-Threatening Ischemia
VL Very Low L Low
M Moderate H High
4. Estimation of 1-Year Amputation Risk
The WIfI clinical stage is determined by the combination of the three parameters (W, I, fI). The SVS expert panel proposed a matrix for estimating the 1-year major amputation risk for each Wound × Ischemia combination, stratified by the infection grade (fI). Table 4 of the original article (MILLS et al., 2014, p. 228) is reproduced below for each infection grade:
For fI = 0 (No infection)
W ↓ / I → I0 I1 I2 I3
W0 VL VL L M
W1 VL VL L M
W2 L L M H
W3 M M H H
