Introduction to Intensive Care Scales
A Intensive Care Unit (ICU) is the hospital environment where the most severely ill patients are concentrated, with imminent risk of death and need for continuous monitoring and complex interventions. In this context, the assessment scales are indispensable tools to quantify severity, predict outcomes, guide therapeutic management, and optimize resource allocation.
Unlike scales used in other specialties—which generally focus on a single organ system—ICU scales are often multisystem , reflecting the complex nature of the critically ill patient, who may present simultaneous dysfunctions in multiple organs.
O APACHE II (Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Evaluation) is the most well-known severity score worldwide. Applied in the first 24 hours of admission, it uses 12 physiological variables, age, and comorbidities to estimate the risk of hospital mortality. The SOFA (Sequential Organ Failure Assessment) assesses dysfunction of six organ systems daily, and is now the main criterion for defining sepsis according to the Sepsis-3 consensus: an increase of 2 or more points in SOFA, associated with infection, defines the septic condition.
In addition to severity scores, the intensive care nurse uses scales to delirium detection (CAM-ICU), neurological assessment in sedated or intubated patients (FOUR, Glasgow), pain assessment in non-verbal patients (BPS — Behavioral Pain Scale) and severity stratification in respiratory infections (CURB-65).
The systematic application of these scales, integrated with the Nursing Care Systematization (SAE), allows early detection of clinical deterioration, precise communication during multidisciplinary rounds, and objective evaluation of response to therapeutic interventions.
When to Use Each Scale in the ICU
Klinische Situatie Schaal Rechtvaardiging
ICU admission — severity assessment APACHE II Predicts hospital mortality and allows comparison of severity between different ICU populations. Apply with the worst values of the first 24 hours.
Daily monitoring of organ dysfunction SOFA Assesses 6 systems daily. Variation ≥ 2 points + infection = sepsis (Sepsis-3). Guides therapeutic adjustments.
Suspected delirium CAM-ICU Detects delirium in critically ill patients, even under mechanical ventilation. Should be applied every shift.
Intubated/sedated patient — neurological assessment FOUR Does not depend on verbal response. Assesses brainstem reflexes and respiratory pattern.
Decreased consciousness in the ICU Glasgow Quick, universally known. Can be complemented by FOUR if patient is intubated.
Pain in patient under mechanical ventilation BPS Assesses facial expression, upper limb movements, and adaptation to mechanical ventilation. Score 3-12.
Severe pneumonia in the ICU CURB-65 Stratifies severity of community-acquired pneumonia. CURB-65 ≥ 3 indicates ICU.
Comparison of the Main ICU Scales
Schaal Wat het Beoordeelt Bereik Frequency Main Use
APACHE II 12 fysiologische variabelen + leeftijd + comorbiditeiten 0 tot 71 Once (admission) Mortality prediction
SOFA 6 organ systems (resp, coag, hep, cardio, neuro, renal) 0 tot 24 Daily Organ dysfunction, sepsis definition
CAM-ICU 4 delirium characteristics Positief/Negatief Every shift Delirium diagnosis
BPS Facial expression, upper limbs, ventilator adaptation 3 to 12 Continuous Pain in non-communicative patient
FOUR Eyes, motor, brainstem, respiration 0 tot 16 Serial Neurological in intubated patients
CURB-65 Confusion, urea, respiration, blood pressure, age 0 tot 5 Admission Pneumonia severity
